[Art. 68(2)] 2. The scientific panel shall consist of experts selected by the Commission on the basis of up-to-date scientific or technical expertise in the field of AI necessary for the tasks set out in paragraph 3, and shall be able to demonstrate meeting all of the following conditions:
(a) having particular expertise and competence and scientific or technical expertise in the field of AI;
(b) independence from any provider of AI systems or general-purpose AI models;
(c) an ability to carry out activities diligently, accurately and objectively.
The Commission, in consultation with the Board, shall determine the number of experts on the panel in accordance with the required needs and shall ensure fair gender and geographical representation.

answer:
Yes. High-risk AI systems must be designed and developed—including with appropriate human-machine interface tools—in such a way that they can be effectively overseen by natural persons during the period in which they are in use [Art. 14(1)]. 

Key details and conditions regarding this supervision include:

* **Purpose:** Human oversight aims to "prevent or minimise the risks to health, safety or fundamental rights" when the system is used as intended or under reasonably foreseeable misuse [Art. 14(2)].
* **Implementation of measures:** Oversight measures must be commensurate with the system's risks, autonomy, and context of use, and must be built into the system by the provider and/or identified by the provider for implementation by the deployer [Art. 14(3)]. Deployers must assign this oversight to natural persons with the "necessary competence, training and authority, as well as the necessary support" [Art. 26(2)].
* **Capabilities of overseers:** Natural persons assigned to oversight must be enabled to:
  * Understand the system’s capacities and limitations and monitor its operation to detect anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * Remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on outputs) [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * Correctly interpret outputs [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * Decide to not use the system, or to disregard, override, or reverse its output [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * Intervene or interrupt operation via a "stop" button or similar procedure to halt the system safely [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Specific verification requirement:** For high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, deployers must ensure that no action or decision is taken based on the system's identification unless it has been separately verified and confirmed by at least two competent, trained natural persons [Art. 14(5)]. This two-person rule does not apply to law enforcement, migration, border control, or asylum uses if Union or national law considers it disproportionate [Art. 14(5)].

