answer:
Yes. Providers of high-risk AI systems are expressly required to keep the documentation referred to in Article 18 [Art. 16(d)]. 

Specifically, documentation record-keeping obligations include:

* **General documentation-keeping obligation and retention period:** The provider must keep certain documentation at the disposal of national competent authorities for a period ending 10 years after the high-risk AI system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)]. This includes:
  * The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)], which must be drawn up before placing the system on the market or putting it into service and kept up to date [Art. 11(1)];
  * Documentation concerning the quality management system referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)], which must also be kept at the disposal of national competent authorities for 10 years after placing on the market or putting into service [Art. 47(1)].

* **Quality management system requirement:** Providers must include "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" within their quality management system [Art. 17(1)(k)].

* **Special rule for financial institutions:** Providers that are financial institutions subject to internal governance requirements under Union financial services law must maintain the technical documentation as part of the documentation kept under that relevant law [Art. 18(3)].

Where a market surveillance authority takes a decision regarding real-world testing (such as suspending, terminating, or requiring modifications to the testing) or issues an objection to it, the decision or objection must indicate [Art. 76(3)], [Art. 76(4)]:

* The grounds for the decision or objection [Art. 76(4)];
* How the provider or prospective provider can challenge the decision or objection [Art. 76(4)].

The European Data Protection Supervisor may impose administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies falling within the scope of the Regulation [Art. 100(1)].

When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and when deciding on its amount in each individual case, "all relevant circumstances of the specific situation shall be taken into account" and due regard must be given to the following factors:

* **Nature and impact:** The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system concerned, as well as, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* **Responsibility:** The degree of responsibility of the Union institution, body, office, or agency, taking into account technical and organisational measures implemented by them [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* **Mitigation:** Any action taken by the Union institution, body, office, or agency to mitigate the damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* **Cooperation:** The degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor to remedy the infringement and mitigate its possible adverse effects, including compliance with any measures previously ordered against them regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* **Prior conduct:** Any similar previous infringements by the Union institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* **Notification:** The manner in which the infringement became known to the European Data Protection Supervisor, particularly whether, and to what extent, the institution, body, office, or agency notified the infringement [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* **Budget:** The annual budget of the Union institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

gold_text:
[Art. 100(1)] 1. The European Data Protection Supervisor may impose administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices and agencies falling within the scope of this Regulation. When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and when deciding on the amount of the administrative fine in each individual case, all relevant circumstances of the specific situation shall be taken into account and due regard shall be given to the following:
(a) the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement and of its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system concerned, as well as, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them;
(b) the degree of responsibility of the Union institution, body, office or agency, taking into account technical and organisational measures implemented by them;
(c) any action taken by the Union institution, body, office or agency to mitigate the damage suffered by affected persons;
(d) the degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor in order to remedy the infringement and mitigate the possible adverse effects of the infringement, including compliance with any of the measures previously ordered by the European Data Protection Supervisor against the Union institution, body, office or agency concerned with regard to the same subject matter;
(e) any similar previous infringements by the Union institution, body, office or agency;
(f) the manner in which the infringement became known to the European Data Protection Supervisor, in particular whether, and if so to what extent, the Union institution, body, office or agency notified the infringement;
(g) the annual budget of the Union institution, body, office or agency.

answer:
Yes. If you are a provider of a high-risk AI system, you are required to establish, implement, document, and maintain a risk management system [Art. 9(1)], [Art. 16(a)], [Art. 16(c)]. 

Under the Act, the criteria and requirements for this process are:
* It must be a "continuous iterative process planned and run throughout the entire lifecycle of a high-risk AI system, requiring regular systematic review and updating" [Art. 9(2)].
* It must comprise the following steps:
  * Identifying and analysing known and reasonably foreseeable risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights when the system is used as intended [Art. 9(2)(a)];
  * Estimating and evaluating risks emerging from intended use and conditions of "reasonably foreseeable misuse" [Art. 9(2)(b)];
  * Evaluating other risks arising from post-market monitoring data gathered under Article 72 [Art. 9(2)(c)];
  * Adopting "appropriate and targeted risk management measures" to address identified risks [Art. 9(2)(d)].
* This risk management system must also be included as an aspect of the provider's quality management system [Art. 16(c)], [Art. 17(1)(g)].

This explanation does not constitute legal advice.

Under the EU AI Act, providers of general-purpose AI models must draw up and keep up to date technical documentation containing, at a minimum, the information set out in Annex XI to be provided upon request to the AI Office and national competent authorities [Art. 53(1)(a)]. 

This documentation must contain, as appropriate to the size and risk profile of the model, the following basic details [Annex XI, Section 1]:

* **A general description of the model**, including [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1]:
  * The tasks the model is intended to perform and the type and nature of AI systems in which it can be integrated [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(a)];
  * Applicable acceptable use policies [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(b)];
  * Date of release and methods of distribution [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(c)];
  * Architecture and number of parameters [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(d)];
  * Modality (e.g. text, image) and format of inputs and outputs [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(e)];
  * The licence [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(f)].

* **Development and model elements**, including [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2]:
  * Technical means required for integration into AI systems (e.g. instructions of use, infrastructure, tools) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(a)];
  * Design specifications of the model and training process (training methodologies, key design choices, rationale, assumptions, what the model optimises for, and parameter relevance) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(b)];
  * Information on data used for training, testing, and validation (type, provenance, curation methodologies, number of data points, scope, characteristics, how data was obtained/selected, and measures to detect unsuitable data sources and identifiable biases) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(c)];
  * Computational resources used (e.g. number of floating point operations), training time, and training details [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(d)];
  * Known or estimated energy consumption (which may be based on computational resources if unknown) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(e)].

If the general-purpose AI model carries **systemic risk**, providers must additionally include [Annex XI, Section 2, points 1–3]:
* A detailed description of evaluation strategies, metrics, criteria, limitations identification, and results [Annex XI, Section 2, point 1];
* Adversarial testing measures (e.g. red teaming) and model adaptations (such as alignment and fine-tuning), where applicable [Annex XI, Section 2, point 2];
* System architecture details explaining how software components integrate and feed into each other, where applicable [Annex XI, Section 2, point 3].

*(Note: Providers must also prepare downstream transparency documentation containing elements set out in Annex XII [Art. 53(1)(b), Annex XII].)*

gold_text:
[Annex XI, Section 1, point 1] 1. A general description of the general-purpose AI model including:
(a) the tasks that the model is intended to perform and the type and nature of AI systems in which it can be integrated;
(b) the acceptable use policies applicable;
(c) the date of release and methods of distribution;
(d) the architecture and number of parameters;
(e) the modality (e.g. text, image) and format of inputs and outputs;
(f) the licence.

answer:
The provided provisions do not address whether testing bodies based outside the EU can be approved to check AI systems. 

The provided excerpts exclusively cover the rules for providers or prospective providers conducting the testing of high-risk AI systems in real-world conditions outside AI regulatory sandboxes [Art. 60(1)], including:
* Establishment requirements, specifying that a provider or prospective provider conducting such testing must be "established in the Union or has appointed a legal representative who is established in the Union" [Art. 60(4)(d)];
* The roles, powers, and approval procedures of market surveillance authorities in Member States [Art. 60(4)(a)], [Art. 60(4)(b)], [Art. 60(6)];
* Internal oversight by providers and deployers [Art. 60(4)(j)]; and
* Rules regarding data transfers to third countries [Art. 60(4)(e)].

Based on the provided excerpts, the provider must ensure logging capabilities and documentation tracking after placing a high-risk AI system on the market or putting it into service:

* **Logging and Traceability:** Logging capabilities must enable the recording of events relevant for:
  * identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk (within the meaning of Article 79(1)) or in a substantial modification [Art. 12(2)(a)];
  * facilitating post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72 [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
  * monitoring the operation of high-risk AI systems referred to in Article 26(5) [Art. 12(2)(c)].
* **Testing and Quality Management:** The quality management system must include examination, test, and validation procedures to be carried out after the development of the system and specify their frequency [Art. 17(1)(d)], as well as systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information [Art. 17(1)(k)].
* **Documentation Retention (10 years):** For a period ending 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service, the provider must keep at the disposal of national competent authorities:
  * the technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * quality management system documentation referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * documentation regarding changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * the EU declaration of conformity [Art. 18(1)(e)], which must be drawn up in written machine-readable, physical, or electronically signed format and submitted to authorities upon request [Art. 47(1)].

**Exceptions:**
* The provided excerpts do not specify any exceptions to the post-release event logging or 10-year documentation retention rules. 
* Regarding pre-market registration in the EU database, an exception exists for "high-risk AI systems referred to in point 2 of Annex III" [Art. 49(1)], and a provider who assesses that an Annex III system is not high-risk is not subject to high-risk classification but must document its assessment and register under Article 49(2) [Art. 6(4)]. The excerpts do not provide other exceptions.

If you are a deployer required to register the use of a high-risk AI system under Article 49(3) (applicable to public authorities, Union institutions, bodies, offices, or agencies, or persons acting on their behalf, for systems listed in Annex III other than point 2) [Art. 49(3)], you must provide and keep up to date the following information in the EU database [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5]:

* The name, address, and contact details of the deployer [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* The name, address, and contact details of the person submitting information on behalf of the deployer [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* The URL of the entry of the AI system in the EU database by its provider [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* A summary of the findings of the fundamental rights impact assessment conducted in accordance with Article 27 [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* Where applicable, a summary of the data protection impact assessment carried out in accordance with Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680 as specified in Article 26(8) [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5].

Additionally, the personal data contained in the database includes the names and contact details of natural persons who are responsible for registering the system and have the legal authority to represent the deployer [Art. 71(5)]. Note that if you find the system has not been registered in the database by its provider, you must not use it and must inform the provider or distributor [Art. 26(8)].

This is not legal advice.

gold_text:
[Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5] Section C — Information to be submitted by deployers of high-risk AI systems in accordance with Article 49(3)
The following information shall be provided and thereafter kept up to date with regard to high-risk AI systems to be registered in accordance with Article 49(3):
1. The name, address and contact details of the deployer;
2. The name, address and contact details of the person submitting information on behalf of the deployer;
3. The URL of the entry of the AI system in the EU database by its provider;
4. A summary of the findings of the fundamental rights impact assessment conducted in accordance with Article 27;
5. A summary of the data protection impact assessment carried out in accordance with Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680 as specified in Article 26(8) of this Regulation, where applicable.

answer:
Yes. National public authorities or bodies that protect fundamental rights are required to treat any information or documentation they obtain pursuant to Article 77 in accordance with the confidentiality obligations set out in Article 78 [Art. 77(4)].

Under these obligations, they must, in accordance with Union or national law, respect the confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out their tasks and activities to protect, in particular [Art. 78(1)]:
* intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets, including source code (except as referred to in Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, specifically for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

gold_text:
[Art. 2(1)] 1. This Regulation applies to:
(a) providers placing on the market or putting into service AI systems or placing on the market general-purpose AI models in the Union, irrespective of whether those providers are established or located within the Union or in a third country;
(b) deployers of AI systems that have their place of establishment or are located within the Union;
(c) providers and deployers of AI systems that have their place of establishment or are located in a third country, where the output produced by the AI system is used in the Union;
(d) importers and distributors of AI systems;
(e) product manufacturers placing on the market or putting into service an AI system together with their product and under their own name or trademark;
(f) authorised representatives of providers, which are not established in the Union;
(g) affected persons that are located in the Union.

answer:
A general-purpose AI model is classified as presenting a systemic risk if it meets either of the following conditions:

* **High impact capabilities:** Evaluated on the basis of appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks [Art. 51(1)(a)]. A model is **presumed** to have high impact capabilities when the cumulative amount of computation used for its training measured in floating point operations is greater than $10^{25}$ [Art. 51(2)].
* **Commission decision (equivalent capabilities or impact):** Based on a decision of the Commission (ex officio or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel), when it has capabilities or an impact equivalent to high impact capabilities, having regard to specific criteria [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)].

For determining equivalent capabilities or impact, the Commission takes into account the following criteria set out in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Annex XIII]:
* The number of parameters of the model [Annex XIII, point (a)];
* The quality or size of the data set (for example, measured through tokens) [Annex XIII, point (b)];
* The amount of computation used for training the model, measured in floating point operations or indicated by a combination of variables such as estimated cost, estimated time, or estimated energy consumption for training [Annex XIII, point (c)];
* The input and output modalities (such as text to text, text to image, multi-modality), state-of-the-art thresholds for determining high-impact capabilities for each modality, and specific input/output types (e.g., biological sequences) [Annex XIII, point (d)];
* Benchmarks and capability evaluations, including the number of tasks performed without additional training, adaptability to learn new and distinct tasks, level of autonomy, scalability, and access to tools [Annex XIII, point (e)];
* Whether it has a high impact on the internal market due to reach, which is presumed when made available to at least 10,000 registered business users established in the Union [Annex XIII, point (f)];
* The number of registered end-users [Annex XIII, point (g)].

In general, systemic risk is defined by whether the model's high-impact capabilities have a significant impact on the Union market due to reach, or actual or reasonably foreseeable negative effects on public health, safety, public security, fundamental rights, or society as a whole, propagating at scale across the value chain [Art. 3(65)].

gold_text:
[Annex XIII] For the purpose of determining that a general-purpose AI model has capabilities or an impact equivalent to those set out in Article 51(1), point (a), the Commission shall take into account the following criteria:
(a) the number of parameters of the model;
(b) the quality or size of the data set, for example measured through tokens;
(c) the amount of computation used for training the model, measured in floating point operations or indicated by a combination of other variables such as estimated cost of training, estimated time required for the training, or estimated energy consumption for the training;
(d) the input and output modalities of the model, such as text to text (large language models), text to image, multi-modality, and the state of the art thresholds for determining high-impact capabilities for each modality, and the specific type of inputs and outputs (e.g. biological sequences);
(e) the benchmarks and evaluations of capabilities of the model, including considering the number of tasks without additional training, adaptability to learn new, distinct tasks, its level of autonomy and scalability, the tools it has access to;
(f) whether it has a high impact on the internal market due to its reach, which shall be presumed when it has been made available to at least 10 000 registered business users established in the Union;
(g) the number of registered end-users.

answer:
Under the EU AI Act, importers must indicate the following information on the high-risk AI system and on its packaging or its accompanying documentation, where applicable:

* Their name, registered trade name, or registered trade mark [Art. 23(3)];
* The address at which they can be contacted [Art. 23(3)].

