answer:
Based on the provided excerpts, a provider has the following obligations regarding its authorised representative:

* **Enabling the representative:** For high-risk AI systems, the provider must "enable its authorised representative to perform the tasks specified in the mandate received from the provider" [Art. 22(2)].
* **Mandating required tasks for high-risk AI systems:** The provider must give a mandate that empowers the authorised representative to:
  * Be addressed by competent authorities, in addition to or instead of the provider, on all compliance issues [Art. 22(3)];
  * Verify that the EU declaration of conformity and technical documentation are drawn up and an appropriate conformity assessment procedure was carried out [Art. 22(3)(a)];
  * Keep available for competent and national authorities (for 10 years after placing on the market or putting into service) the provider's contact details, the EU declaration of conformity, technical documentation, and any notified body certificates [Art. 22(3)(b)];
  * Provide competent authorities, upon reasoned request, with necessary conformity information and documentation, including access to automatically generated logs to the extent they are under the provider's control [Art. 22(3)(c)];
  * Cooperate with competent authorities to reduce and mitigate risks posed by the system [Art. 22(3)(d)];
  * Comply with registration obligations under Article 49(1), or, if the provider registers itself, ensure that the information referred to in point 3 of Section A of Annex VIII is correct [Art. 22(3)(e)].
* **Mandating required tasks for general-purpose AI models:** The provider must establish a mandate empowering the authorised representative to:
  * Verify that Annex XI technical documentation is drawn up and obligations under Article 53 (and Article 55, where applicable) are fulfilled [Art. 54(3)(a)];
  * Keep a copy of the Annex XI technical documentation and the provider's contact details at the disposal of the AI Office and national competent authorities for 10 years after the model has been placed on the market [Art. 54(3)(b)];
  * Provide the AI Office, upon reasoned request, with all documentation and information needed to demonstrate compliance [Art. 54(3)(c)];
  * Cooperate with the AI Office and competent authorities in any actions taken regarding the model [Art. 54(3)(d)].

Upon a request by national competent authorities, providers of high-risk AI systems must provide or make accessible the following documentation and information:

* **Conformity information and documentation:** All the information and documentation necessary to demonstrate the conformity of the high-risk AI system with the requirements set out in Section 2, provided upon a reasoned request in a language easily understood by the authority in one of the official languages of the Union institutions indicated by the Member State concerned [Art. 21(1)], [Art. 16(k)].
* **EU declaration of conformity:** A copy of the written EU declaration of conformity must be submitted upon request [Art. 47(1)]. Providers must also keep this declaration at the disposal of national competent authorities for 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 47(1)], [Art. 18(1)(e)].
* **Automatically generated logs:** Access to the automatically generated logs referred to in Article 12(1), upon a reasoned request, to the extent such logs are under their control [Art. 21(2)].
* **Documentation kept at disposal:** For a period ending 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service, providers must keep at the disposal of national competent authorities [Art. 18(1)]:
  * The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * Documentation concerning the quality management system referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)].

Under Article 66(e) of the AI Act, at the request of the Commission or on its own initiative, the Board may issue recommendations and written opinions on "any relevant matters related to the implementation of this Regulation and to its consistent and effective application" [Art. 66(e)]. 

This specifically includes:
* The development and application of codes of conduct and codes of practice under the Regulation, as well as the Commission’s guidelines [Art. 66(e)(i)];
* The evaluation and review of the Regulation under Article 112, including regarding:
  * serious incident reports referred to in Article 73,
  * the functioning of the EU database referred to in Article 71,
  * the preparation of delegated or implementing acts, and
  * possible alignments of the Regulation with the Union harmonisation legislation listed in Annex I [Art. 66(e)(ii)];
* Technical specifications or existing standards concerning the requirements set out in Chapter III, Section 2 [Art. 66(e)(iii)];
* The use of harmonised standards or common specifications referred to in Articles 40 and 41 [Art. 66(e)(iv)];
* Trends such as European global competitiveness in AI, the uptake of AI in the Union, and digital skills development [Art. 66(e)(v)];
* Trends on the evolving typology of AI value chains, particularly regarding resulting implications for accountability [Art. 66(e)(vi)];
* The potential need for amendments to Annex III under Article 7, and the potential need for possible revision of Article 5 under Article 112, taking into account available evidence and the latest technological developments [Art. 66(e)(vii)].

Additionally, the Board may provide opinions to the Commission on qualified alerts regarding general-purpose AI models [Art. 66(n)].

gold_text:
[Art. 66(e)] The Board shall advise and assist the Commission and the Member States in order to facilitate the consistent and effective application of this Regulation. To that end, the Board may in particular:
(e) at the request of the Commission or on its own initiative, issue recommendations and written opinions on any relevant matters related to the implementation of this Regulation and to its consistent and effective application, including:
(i) on the development and application of codes of conduct and codes of practice pursuant to this Regulation, as well as of the Commission’s guidelines;
(ii) the evaluation and review of this Regulation pursuant to Article 112, including as regards the serious incident reports referred to in Article 73, and the functioning of the EU database referred to in Article 71, the preparation of the delegated or implementing acts, and as regards possible alignments of this Regulation with the Union harmonisation legislation listed in Annex I;
(iii) on technical specifications or existing standards regarding the requirements set out in Chapter III, Section 2;
(iv) on the use of harmonised standards or common specifications referred to in Articles 40 and 41;
(v) trends, such as European global competitiveness in AI, the uptake of AI in the Union, and the development of digital skills;
(vi) trends on the evolving typology of AI value chains, in particular on the resulting implications in terms of accountability;
(vii) on the potential need for amendment to Annex III in accordance with Article 7, and on the potential need for possible revision of Article 5 pursuant to Article 112, taking into account relevant available evidence and the latest developments in technology;

answer:
Under Article 10(5) of the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, providers of high-risk AI systems may process special categories of personal data exceptionally and only "to the extent that it is strictly necessary for the purpose of ensuring bias detection and correction in relation to the high-risk AI systems in accordance with paragraph (2), points (f) and (g)" [Art. 10(5)].

Such processing is subject to appropriate safeguards for fundamental rights and freedoms, and must comply with Regulations (EU) 2016/679 and (EU) 2018/1725 and Directive (EU) 2016/680 [Art. 10(5)]. In addition, **all** of the following conditions must be met [Art. 10(5)]:

* **Non-substitutability:** the bias detection and correction cannot be effectively fulfilled by processing other data, including synthetic or anonymised data [Art. 10(5)(a)];
* **Security and privacy measures:** the data are subject to technical limitations on re-use, as well as state-of-the-art security and privacy-preserving measures, including pseudonymisation [Art. 10(5)(b)];
* **Strict access controls:** the data are secured, protected, and subject to suitable safeguards, including strict controls and documentation of access, to prevent misuse and ensure that only authorised persons subject to confidentiality obligations have access [Art. 10(5)(c)];
* **No third-party transfer:** the data are not to be transmitted, transferred, or otherwise accessed by other parties [Art. 10(5)(d)];
* **Data deletion:** the data are deleted once the bias has been corrected or the data reaches the end of its retention period, whichever comes first [Art. 10(5)(e)];
* **Documentation:** records of processing activities include the specific reasons why processing this data was strictly necessary to detect and correct biases and why that objective could not be achieved using other data [Art. 10(5)(f)].

Where a high-risk AI system does not use techniques involving the training of AI models, these provisions apply only to the testing data sets [Art. 10(6)].

gold_text:
[Art. 10(5)] 5. To the extent that it is strictly necessary for the purpose of ensuring bias detection and correction in relation to the high-risk AI systems in accordance with paragraph (2), points (f) and (g) of this Article, the providers of such systems may exceptionally process special categories of personal data, subject to appropriate safeguards for the fundamental rights and freedoms of natural persons. In addition to the provisions set out in Regulations (EU) 2016/679 and (EU) 2018/1725 and Directive (EU) 2016/680, all the following conditions must be met in order for such processing to occur:
(a) the bias detection and correction cannot be effectively fulfilled by processing other data, including synthetic or anonymised data;
(b) the special categories of personal data are subject to technical limitations on the re-use of the personal data, and state-of-the-art security and privacy-preserving measures, including pseudonymisation;
(c) the special categories of personal data are subject to measures to ensure that the personal data processed are secured, protected, subject to suitable safeguards, including strict controls and documentation of the access, to avoid misuse and ensure that only authorised persons have access to those personal data with appropriate confidentiality obligations;
(d) the special categories of personal data are not to be transmitted, transferred or otherwise accessed by other parties;
(e) the special categories of personal data are deleted once the bias has been corrected or the personal data has reached the end of its retention period, whichever comes first;
(f) the records of processing activities pursuant to Regulations (EU) 2016/679 and (EU) 2018/1725 and Directive (EU) 2016/680 include the reasons why the processing of special categories of personal data was strictly necessary to detect and correct biases, and why that objective could not be achieved by processing other data.

answer:
Under the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, high-risk AI systems must technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the system's lifetime [Art. 12(1)]. 

To ensure traceability appropriate to the system's intended purpose, logging capabilities must enable the recording of events relevant for:
* "identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification" [Art. 12(2)(a)];
* "facilitating the post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72" [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
* "monitoring the operation of high-risk AI systems referred to in Article 26(5)" [Art. 12(2)(c)].

Additionally, for high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, logging capabilities must provide, at a minimum, the recording of:
* the period of each use (start date and time, and end date and time) [Art. 12(3)(a)];
* the reference database against which input data has been checked [Art. 12(3)(b)];
* the input data for which the search led to a match [Art. 12(3)(c)]; and
* the identification of the natural persons involved in verifying the results under Article 14(5) [Art. 12(3)(d)].

