answer:
Under the EU AI Act, if a provider of a high-risk AI system or its authorised representative established on a Member State's territory goes bankrupt or ceases its activity before the mandatory 10-year storage period ends:

* **Member State rules apply:** "Each Member State shall determine conditions under which the documentation referred to in paragraph 1 remains at the disposal of the national competent authorities for the period indicated in that paragraph" [Art. 18(2)].
* **Documentation covered:** This refers to the documentation kept for 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)], specifically:
  * Technical documentation [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * Quality management system documentation [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity [Art. 18(1)(e)].

When the Commission requests information from a general-purpose AI model provider under Article 91, the request must state and include the following details [Art. 91(4)]:

* The legal basis of the request [Art. 91(4)];
* The purpose of the request [Art. 91(4)];
* A specification of what information is required [Art. 91(4)];
* A set period within which the information is to be provided [Art. 91(4)]; and
* An indication of the fines provided for in Article 101 for supplying incorrect, incomplete, or misleading information [Art. 91(4)].

Yes. When performing their tasks, national competent authorities, market surveillance authorities, and national public authorities or bodies protecting fundamental rights must act in accordance with the confidentiality obligations set out in Article 78 [Art. 70(5)], [Art. 77(4)], [Art. 78(1)].

Under these obligations:
* Authorities must, in accordance with Union or national law, respect the confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out their tasks and activities to protect, in particular:
  * Intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets, including source code (except in cases under Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
  * The effective implementation of the Regulation (especially for inspections, investigations, or audits) [Art. 78(1)(b)];
  * Public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
  * The conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
  * Information classified pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].
* Information exchanged on a confidential basis between national competent authorities, or between them and the Commission, must not be disclosed without prior consultation of the originating authority and the deployer when high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1, 6, or 7 of Annex III are used by law enforcement, border control, immigration, or asylum authorities and disclosure would jeopardise public and national security interests [Art. 78(3)].
* Such exchanges of information must not cover sensitive operational data related to the activities of law enforcement, border control, immigration, or asylum authorities [Art. 78(3)]. 
* Where law enforcement, immigration, or asylum authorities provide such systems, technical documentation must remain on their premises, and only market surveillance authority staff holding the appropriate level of security clearance are permitted access to it or its copies [Art. 78(3)].

