8. The status of the AI system (on the market, or in service; no longer placed on the market/in service, recalled);
9. Any Member States in which the AI system has been placed on the market, put into service or made available in the Union.

answer:
Under Article 112(10), when submitting appropriate proposals to amend this Regulation, the Commission shall in particular take into account [Art. 112(10)]:

* developments in technology [Art. 112(10)];
* the effect of AI systems on health and safety [Art. 112(10)];
* the effect of AI systems on fundamental rights [Art. 112(10)];
* the state of progress in the information society [Art. 112(10)].

Under Article 89(2), downstream providers have the right to lodge a complaint alleging an infringement of the Regulation [Art. 89(2)]. The complaint must be duly reasoned and indicate at least:

* The point of contact of the provider of the general-purpose AI model concerned [Art. 89(2)(a)];
* A description of the relevant facts, the provisions of the Regulation concerned, and the reason why the downstream provider considers that the provider of the general-purpose AI model concerned infringed the Regulation [Art. 89(2)(b)];
* Any other information that the downstream provider considers relevant, including, where appropriate, information gathered on its own initiative [Art. 89(2)(c)].

gold_text:
[Art. 89(2)] 2. Downstream providers shall have the right to lodge a complaint alleging an infringement of this Regulation. A complaint shall be duly reasoned and indicate at least:
(a) the point of contact of the provider of the general-purpose AI model concerned;
(b) a description of the relevant facts, the provisions of this Regulation concerned, and the reason why the downstream provider considers that the provider of the general-purpose AI model concerned infringed this Regulation;
(c) any other information that the downstream provider that sent the request considers relevant, including, where appropriate, information gathered on its own initiative.

answer:
Yes, providers or prospective providers are required to register the testing in real world conditions before conducting it outside AI regulatory sandboxes [Art. 60(4)(c)]. 

Depending on the type of high-risk AI system listed in Annex III, registration must be carried out as follows:

* **Standard Annex III systems:** The provider or prospective provider must register the testing in real world conditions in accordance with Article 71(4) with a Union-wide unique single identification number and with the information specified in Annex IX [Art. 60(4)(c)].
* **Law enforcement, migration, asylum, and border control systems (points 1, 6, and 7 of Annex III):** The provider or prospective provider must register the testing in the "secure non-public section of the EU database according to Article 49(4), point (d), with a Union-wide unique single identification number and with the information specified therein" [Art. 60(4)(c)].
* **Critical infrastructure systems (point 2 of Annex III):** The provider or prospective provider must register the testing in real world conditions in accordance with Article 49(5) [Art. 60(4)(c)].

When registering under Annex IX, the information submitted must include:
* A Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing [Annex IX, point 1];
* Name and contact details of the provider/prospective provider and deployers involved [Annex IX, point 2];
* A brief description of the AI system, its intended purpose, and identification information [Annex IX, point 3];
* A summary of the main characteristics of the real-world testing plan [Annex IX, point 4];
* Information on the suspension or termination of the testing [Annex IX, point 5].

An authorised representative must keep the documentation for a high-risk AI system for a period of **10 years** after the system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 22(3)(b)].

Specifically, their mandate empowers them to keep at the disposal of competent authorities and relevant national authorities or bodies:
* the contact details of the provider that appointed them [Art. 22(3)(b)];
* a copy of the EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 22(3)(b)];
* the technical documentation [Art. 22(3)(b)]; and
* if applicable, the certificate issued by the notified body [Art. 22(3)(b)].

Based on the provided excerpts:

**Who sets up the scientific panel:**
The Commission establishes the scientific panel of independent experts by means of an implementing act adopted in accordance with the examination procedure referred to in Article 98(2) [Art. 68(1)]. The experts are selected by the Commission, which also determines their number in consultation with the Board [Art. 68(2)].

**Purpose of the scientific panel:**
The scientific panel is established to "support the enforcement activities under this Regulation" [Art. 68(1)]. Specifically, it advises and supports the AI Office and other authorities in the following tasks [Art. 68(3)]:
* Supporting the implementation and enforcement of the Regulation regarding general-purpose AI models and systems, which includes:
  * Alerting the AI Office of possible systemic risks at Union level under Article 90 [Art. 68(3)(a)(i)], including issuing qualified alerts where it suspects a model poses a concrete identifiable risk at Union level or meets the conditions in Article 51 [Art. 90(1)];
  * Contributing to developing tools and methodologies (including benchmarks) for evaluating model and system capabilities [Art. 68(3)(a)(ii)];
  * Providing advice on classifying general-purpose AI models with systemic risk and classifying various general-purpose AI models and systems [Art. 68(3)(a)(iii), (iv)];
  * Contributing to the development of tools and templates [Art. 68(3)(a)(v)];
* Supporting the work of market surveillance authorities at their request [Art. 68(3)(b)];
* Supporting cross-border market surveillance activities under Article 74(11) [Art. 68(3)(c)];
* Supporting the AI Office in its duties regarding the Union safeguard procedure under Article 81 [Art. 68(3)(d)].

gold_text:
[Art. 1(2)] 2. This Regulation lays down:
(a) harmonised rules for the placing on the market, the putting into service, and the use of AI systems in the Union;
(b) prohibitions of certain AI practices;
(c) specific requirements for high-risk AI systems and obligations for operators of such systems;
(d) harmonised transparency rules for certain AI systems;
(e) harmonised rules for the placing on the market of general-purpose AI models;
(f) rules on market monitoring, market surveillance, governance and enforcement;
(g) measures to support innovation, with a particular focus on SMEs, including start-ups.

answer:
Under the EU AI Act, the role of the scientific panel of independent experts is to advise and support the AI Office [Art. 68(3)]. 

Its duties and role in relation to the AI Office include:

* **Supporting implementation and enforcement regarding general-purpose AI models and systems** [Art. 68(3)(a)], in particular by:
  * Alerting the AI Office of possible systemic risks at Union level of general-purpose AI models under Article 90 [Art. 68(3)(a)(i)], specifically by providing a "qualified alert" when it suspects a model poses a concrete identifiable risk at Union level or meets the conditions in Article 51 [Art. 90(1)]. Such an alert allows the Commission, through the AI Office, to exercise supervisory and enforcement powers to assess the matter [Art. 90(2)];
  * Contributing to the development of tools and methodologies for evaluating capabilities of general-purpose AI models and systems, including through benchmarks [Art. 68(3)(a)(ii)];
  * Providing advice on the classification of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk [Art. 68(3)(a)(iii)];
  * Providing advice on the classification of various general-purpose AI models and systems [Art. 68(3)(a)(iv)];
  * Contributing to the development of tools and templates [Art. 68(3)(a)(v)].
* **Supporting safeguard procedures**: Supporting the AI Office in carrying out its duties in the context of the Union safeguard procedure pursuant to Article 81 [Art. 68(3)(d)].
* **Requesting assistance and information**: Requesting the assistance of the AI Office for the performance of its tasks [Art. 68(5)], and submitting duly substantiated requests to the Commission to issue requests for information to providers of general-purpose AI models when necessary and proportionate for fulfilling its tasks [Art. 91(3)].
* **Operational independence and conflict management**: The experts must perform their tasks with impartiality, objectivity, and confidentiality, and neither seek nor take instructions from anyone [Art. 68(4)]. In turn, the AI Office must establish systems and procedures to actively manage and prevent potential conflicts of interest concerning the panel's experts [Art. 68(4)]. 

*(Note: References in the Regulation to the AI Office are construed as references to the Commission [Art. 3(47)]).*

