answer:
Under the EU AI Act, providers of high-risk AI systems must implement specific procedures, governance practices, and documentation regarding data collection and handling:

* **Quality Management System (QMS) Procedures:** Providers must establish a quality management system documented in written policies, procedures, and instructions [Art. 17(1)]. This must include:
  * "systems and procedures for data management, including data acquisition, data collection, data analysis, data labelling, data storage, data filtration, data mining, data aggregation, data retention and any other operation regarding the data" carried out before and for placing the system on the market or putting it into service [Art. 17(1)(f)].
  * "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" [Art. 17(1)(k)].

* **Data Governance and Management Practices:** Whenever high-risk AI systems use techniques involving training models with data, the training, validation, and testing data sets must undergo data governance and management practices appropriate for their intended purpose [Art. 10(1), Art. 10(2)]. These practices must specifically concern:
  * Relevant design choices [Art. 10(2)(a)];
  * "data collection processes and the origin of data, and in the case of personal data, the original purpose of the data collection" [Art. 10(2)(b)];
  * Data-preparation processing operations (such as annotation, labelling, cleaning, updating, enrichment, and aggregation) [Art. 10(2)(c)];
  * Formulation of assumptions regarding what data are supposed to measure and represent [Art. 10(2)(d)];
  * Assessment of data availability, quantity, and suitability [Art. 10(2)(e)];
  * Examination for biases that may affect health, safety, or fundamental rights, or lead to unlawful discrimination, along with appropriate measures to detect, prevent, and mitigate those biases [Art. 10(2)(f), Art. 10(2)(g)];
  * Identification and addressing of data gaps or shortcomings [Art. 10(2)(h)];
  * Taking into account the particular characteristics of the specific geographical, contextual, behavioural, or functional setting of intended use [Art. 10(4)].

* **Technical and QMS Documentation Keeping:** 
  * Providers must draw up technical documentation demonstrating compliance before the system is placed on the market or put into service, containing at least the elements set out in Annex IV, and keep it up to date [Art. 11(1)]. SMEs and start-ups may provide these elements in a simplified manner using a form established by the Commission [Art. 11(1)].
  * Providers are obligated to keep this technical documentation and the QMS documentation at the disposal of national competent authorities for a period ending 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 16(c), Art. 16(d), Art. 18(1)(a), Art. 18(1)(b)].

