answer:
The main purpose and objective of the EU AI Act is to:

* Improve the functioning of the internal market [Art. 1(1)], [Recital 1], [Recital 176];
* Promote the uptake of human-centric and trustworthy artificial intelligence (AI) [Art. 1(1)], [Recital 1], [Recital 176];
* Ensure a high level of protection of health, safety, and fundamental rights enshrined in the Charter, including democracy, the rule of law, and environmental protection, against the harmful effects of AI systems in the Union [Art. 1(1)], [Recital 1], [Recital 176]; and
* Support innovation [Art. 1(1)], [Recital 1], [Recital 176].

Additionally, the rules aim to ensure the smooth functioning of the internal market and the cross-border free movement of AI-based goods and services [Recital 1], [Recital 8], support the European human-centric approach to AI, and establish the Union as a global leader in secure, trustworthy, and ethical AI [Recital 8].

Under the EU AI Act, the logging capabilities of high-risk AI systems must enable the recording of events relevant for:

* "identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification" [Art. 12(2)(a)];
* "facilitating the post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72" [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
* "monitoring the operation of high-risk AI systems referred to in Article 26(5)" [Art. 12(2)(c)].

Additionally, for high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, the logging capabilities must provide, at a minimum, the recording of:
* the period of each use of the system (start date and time, and end date and time of each use) [Art. 12(3)(a)];
* the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system [Art. 12(3)(b)];
* the input data for which the search has led to a match [Art. 12(3)(c)]; and
* the identification of the natural persons involved in verifying the results under Article 14(5) [Art. 12(3)(d)].

gold_text:
[Art. 53(1)] 1. Providers of general-purpose AI models shall:
(a) draw up and keep up-to-date the technical documentation of the model, including its training and testing process and the results of its evaluation, which shall contain, at a minimum, the information set out in Annex XI for the purpose of providing it, upon request, to the AI Office and the national competent authorities;
(b) draw up, keep up-to-date and make available information and documentation to providers of AI systems who intend to integrate the general-purpose AI model into their AI systems. Without prejudice to the need to observe and protect intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets in accordance with Union and national law, the information and documentation shall:
(i) enable providers of AI systems to have a good understanding of the capabilities and limitations of the general-purpose AI model and to comply with their obligations pursuant to this Regulation; and
(ii) contain, at a minimum, the elements set out in Annex XII;
(c) put in place a policy to comply with Union law on copyright and related rights, and in particular to identify and comply with, including through state-of-the-art technologies, a reservation of rights expressed pursuant to Article 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790;
(d) draw up and make publicly available a sufficiently detailed summary about the content used for training of the general-purpose AI model, according to a template provided by the AI Office.

[Art. 13(3)(b)] 3. The instructions for use shall contain at least the following information:
(b) the characteristics, capabilities and limitations of performance of the high-risk AI system, including:
(i) its intended purpose;
(ii) the level of accuracy, including its metrics, robustness and cybersecurity referred to in Article 15 against which the high-risk AI system has been tested and validated and which can be expected, and any known and foreseeable circumstances that may have an impact on that expected level of accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity;
(iii) any known or foreseeable circumstance, related to the use of the high-risk AI system in accordance with its intended purpose or under conditions of reasonably foreseeable misuse, which may lead to risks to the health and safety or fundamental rights referred to in Article 9(2);
(iv) where applicable, the technical capabilities and characteristics of the high-risk AI system to provide information that is relevant to explain its output;
(v) when appropriate, its performance regarding specific persons or groups of persons on which the system is intended to be used;
(vi) when appropriate, specifications for the input data, or any other relevant information in terms of the training, validation and testing data sets used, taking into account the intended purpose of the high-risk AI system;
(vii) where applicable, information to enable deployers to interpret the output of the high-risk AI system and use it appropriately;

answer:
The Commission decides to classify and include a general-purpose AI model on the list of models with systemic risk based on the following rules and procedures [Art. 51(1)], [Art. 52(6)]:

* **Models with high-impact capabilities:** A model is classified as having systemic risk if it has high-impact capabilities evaluated using appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks [Art. 51(1)(a)].
  * Providers must notify the Commission without delay (and within two weeks) when their model meets or is known to meet this condition [Art. 52(1)].
  * If the Commission becomes aware of a model presenting systemic risks that has not been notified, it may decide to designate it as a model with systemic risk [Art. 52(1)].
  * If a provider submits arguments pursuant to paragraph 2 that the model does not present systemic risks, but the Commission concludes those arguments are not sufficiently substantiated, the Commission rejects the arguments, and the model is considered to be a model with systemic risk [Art. 52(3)].

* **Commission designation based on equivalent impact and Annex XIII criteria:** The Commission may decide—either *ex officio* or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel—to designate a model as presenting systemic risks if it has capabilities or an impact equivalent to high-impact capabilities [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)]. To determine this, the Commission must take into account the criteria set out in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)], [Annex XIII]:
  * Number of parameters [Annex XIII(a)];
  * Quality or size of the dataset (e.g., measured through tokens) [Annex XIII(b)];
  * Amount of computation used for training (measured in FLOPs or indicated by variables like estimated cost, time, or energy consumption) [Annex XIII(c)];
  * Input and output modalities (e.g., large language models, text-to-image, multi-modality), state-of-the-art thresholds for each modality, and specific input/output types [Annex XIII(d)];
  * Benchmarks and capability evaluations (e.g., tasks performed without additional training, adaptability, autonomy, scalability, and tools accessed) [Annex XIII(e)];
  * Internal market reach and impact, which is presumed if made available to at least 10,000 registered business users established in the Union [Annex XIII(f)];
  * Number of registered end-users [Annex XIII(g)].

* **Reassessment:** A designated provider may request a reassessment with new, objective, and detailed reasons at the earliest six months after the designation decision (or six months after a decision to maintain designation), upon which the Commission may decide to reassess whether the model still presents systemic risks under Annex XIII [Art. 52(5)].

The Commission publishes and keeps updated the resulting list of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk, subject to the protection of intellectual property, confidential business information, or trade secrets [Art. 52(6)].

Under the provided excerpts of the EU AI Act, market surveillance authorities are triggered to take measures against an AI system in the following situations:

* **Presentation of a risk:** When an authority has sufficient reason to consider that an AI system presents a risk (giving particular attention to vulnerable groups and risks to fundamental rights) [Art. 79(2)], or finds that even though a high-risk AI system complies with the Regulation, it "nevertheless presents a risk to the health or safety of persons, to fundamental rights, or to other aspects of public interest protection" [Art. 82(1)].
* **Non-compliance with requirements and obligations:** When an evaluation reveals that the AI system fails to comply with the requirements and obligations laid down in the Regulation [Art. 79(2)]. Specific grounds for non-compliance noted when taking provisional measures include:
  * Non-compliance with prohibited AI practices referred to in Article 5 [Art. 79(6)(a)];
  * A failure of a high-risk AI system to meet the requirements set out in Chapter III, Section 2 [Art. 79(6)(b)];
  * Shortcomings in harmonised standards or common specifications conferring a presumption of conformity [Art. 79(6)(c)];
  * Non-compliance with Article 50 [Art. 79(6)(d)].
* **Failure to take corrective actions:** When an operator fails to take adequate corrective action within the prescribed period (within the shorter of 15 working days, or as provided for in relevant Union harmonisation legislation) after being evaluated and found non-compliant [Art. 79(2), Art. 79(5)].
* **Misclassification as non-high-risk:** When a provider has classified an AI system as non-high-risk pursuant to Article 6(3), but the authority has sufficient reason to consider that it is indeed high-risk, and confirms through evaluation that it is high-risk [Art. 80(1), Art. 80(2)].
* **Persistent formal non-compliance:** When formal non-compliance referred to in Article 83(1) persists [Art. 83(2)].

