answer:
The provided provisions do not address mandatory warranties for AI-enabled smart fridges (or any consumer warranty periods). 

The provided excerpts instead cover:
* The subject matter and scope of the Regulation [Art. 1(2)];
* Obligations to keep documents (such as the EU declaration of conformity, certificates, and technical documentation) at the disposal of authorities for 10 years [Art. 47(1)], [Art. 22(3)(b)], [Art. 23(5)];
* The validity and extension periods for certificates of high-risk AI systems [Art. 44(2)];
* Timeframes for national market surveillance procedures and objections [Art. 79(8)]; and
* Transition periods, entry into force, and application dates of the Regulation [Art. 111(3)], [Art. 113].

The provided provisions do not address or set any royalty rates for artists whose works are used to train AI models. 

Instead, the provided excerpts cover:
* The general purpose of the Regulation [Art. 1(1)];
* The requirement for providers of general-purpose AI models to put in place a policy to comply with Union copyright law—including identifying and complying with reservations of rights expressed under Article 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790—and to publish a sufficiently detailed summary of the training content [Art. 53(1)(c), (d)];
* Principles regarding copyright, text and data mining exceptions, and the need to obtain rightsholder authorization when an opt-out has been expressly reserved [Recital 105];
* The definition of "training data" [Art. 3(29)];
* Prohibitions on untargeted scraping of facial images [Art. 5(1)(e)];
* The definition of "AI literacy" [Art. 3(56)];
* Amendments to Directive 2014/90/EU [Art. 105]; and
* Criteria for designating general-purpose AI models with systemic risk [Annex XIII].

Yes. Providers of high-risk AI systems are required to put a quality management system in place that ensures compliance with the Regulation [Art. 17(1)(a)]. 

This system must be documented "in a systematic and orderly manner in the form of written policies, procedures and instructions" [Art. 17(1)(a)], and it must cover at least the following aspects:

* A strategy for regulatory compliance, including conformity assessment procedures and management of modifications [Art. 17(1)(a)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for the design, design control, and design verification of the system [Art. 17(1)(b)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for development, quality control, and quality assurance [Art. 17(1)(c)];
* Systems and procedures for data management (such as data collection, labelling, storage, retention, etc.) performed before and for placing on the market or putting into service [Art. 17(1)(f)];
* The risk management system referred to in Article 9 [Art. 17(1)(g)];
* Setting up, implementing, and maintaining a post-market monitoring system in accordance with Article 72 [Art. 17(1)(h)];
* Systems and procedures for record-keeping of relevant documentation and information [Art. 17(1)(k)];
* An accountability framework detailing the responsibilities of management and staff regarding all of these aspects [Art. 17(1)(m)].

Under the EU AI Act, high-risk AI systems must meet the following human oversight requirements:

* **Design and Purpose:** Systems must be designed and developed—including with appropriate human-machine interface tools—so that they can be effectively overseen by natural persons while in use [Art. 14(1)]. The oversight aims to prevent or minimise risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights emerging from intended use or reasonably foreseeable misuse, particularly where risks persist despite other requirements [Art. 14(2)].
* **Types of Oversight Measures:** Measures must be commensurate with the risks, level of autonomy, and context of use, and must be ensured through either or both [Art. 14(3)]:
  * Measures built into the system by the provider before placing it on the market or putting it into service (when technically feasible) [Art. 14(3)(a)];
  * Measures identified by the provider prior to market placement or putting into service that are appropriate for the deployer to implement [Art. 14(3)(b)].
* **Enabling the Overseer:** The system must be provided to deployers such that individuals assigned to oversight are enabled, as appropriate and proportionate, to [Art. 14(4)]:
  * Understand the system’s capacities and limitations, and duly monitor operation to detect and address anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * Remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on system outputs), especially where systems provide information or recommendations [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * Correctly interpret system outputs, taking into account available interpretation tools and methods [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * Decide to not use the system, or to disregard, override, or reverse its output in any situation [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * Intervene in operation or interrupt the system via a "stop" button or similar procedure to bring it to a safe halt [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Deployer Obligations:** Deployers must assign oversight to natural persons who possess the necessary competence, training, authority, and support [Art. 26(2)].
* **Instructions for Use:** Instructions for use provided to deployers must include the human oversight measures, including technical measures facilitating output interpretation [Art. 13(3)(d)].
* **Specific Rule for Point 1(a) of Annex III Systems:** For these systems, deployers must not take action or decisions based on the system's identification unless it has been separately verified and confirmed by at least two natural persons with the necessary competence, training, and authority [Art. 14(5)]. This two-person verification requirement does not apply to systems used for law enforcement, migration, border control, or asylum where Union or national law considers it disproportionate [Art. 14(5)].

Selon l'AI Act, un système d'IA est considéré comme à haut risque (« high-risk ») s'il répond aux critères définis à l'Article 6 :

1. **Systèmes d'IA liés à des produits réglementés de l'Union** : Un système d'IA est considéré comme à haut risque lorsque les deux conditions suivantes sont réunies [Art. 6(1)] :
   - Le système d'IA est destiné à être utilisé comme composant de sécurité d'un produit, ou est lui-même un produit, couvert par la législation d'harmonisation de l'Union figurant à l'annexe I [Art. 6(1)(a)] ;
   - Le produit dont le système d'IA est un composant de sécurité, ou le système d'IA lui-même en tant que produit, est soumis à une évaluation de la conformité par un tiers en vue de sa mise sur le marché ou de sa mise en service conformément à cette législation [Art. 6(1)(b)]. Les produits visés comprennent notamment les machines, jouets, ascenseurs, dispositifs médicaux ou l'automobile [Recital 50].

2. **Systèmes d'IA énumérés à l'Annexe III** : En plus de ceux mentionnés ci-dessus, les systèmes d'IA visés à l'Annexe III sont considérés comme à haut risque [Art. 6(2)], [Annex III]. 
   - Par exemple, dans le domaine de l'accès et du bénéfice des services privés essentiels et des services et prestations publics essentiels [Annex III, point 5] :
     - Les systèmes utilisés pour évaluer l'éligibilité des personnes physiques aux prestations et services d'aide publique essentiels (y compris la santé), ou pour les octroyer, réduire, révoquer ou récupérer [Annex III, point 5(a)] ;
     - Les systèmes servant à évaluer la solvabilité ou établir la notation de crédit de personnes physiques (sauf détection des fraudes financières) [Annex III, point 5(b)] ;
     - Les systèmes servant à l'évaluation des risques et à la tarification concernant les personnes physiques en assurance vie et santé [Annex III, point 5(c)] ;
     - Les systèmes servant à évaluer/classer les appels d'urgence ou à réguler/établir la priorité dans l'envoi des services d'intervention d'urgence (police, pompiers, secours médicaux, tri des patients) [Annex III, point 5(d)].

**Exception et documentation** :
Un fournisseur qui estime qu'un système d'IA visé à l'Annexe III n'est pas à haut risque doit documenter son évaluation avant la mise sur le marché ou la mise en service, et est soumis à l'obligation d'enregistrement prévue à l'article 49(2) [Art. 6(4)].

According to the EU AI Act, a ‘general-purpose AI model’ is defined as an AI model that:

* displays significant generality [Art. 3(63)];
* is capable of competently performing a wide range of distinct tasks regardless of the way the model is placed on the market [Art. 3(63)];
* can be integrated into a variety of downstream systems or applications [Art. 3(63)]; and
* includes models trained with a large amount of data using self-supervision at scale [Art. 3(63)].

**Exception:** It does not include AI models that are used for research, development, or prototyping activities before they are placed on the market [Art. 3(63)].

Under the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, an ‘AI regulatory sandbox’ is defined as a controlled framework set up by a competent authority which offers providers or prospective providers of AI systems the possibility to develop, train, validate and test—where appropriate in real-world conditions—an innovative AI system, pursuant to a sandbox plan for a limited time under regulatory supervision [Art. 3(55)].

Key characteristics and functions established by the Act include:

* **Environment and Operation:** It provides a controlled environment that fosters innovation and facilitates the development, training, testing, and validation of innovative AI systems for a limited time before they are placed on the market or put into service, pursuant to a specific sandbox plan agreed between the providers (or prospective providers) and the competent authority [Art. 57(5)]. It may include supervised testing in real-world conditions [Art. 57(5)], subject to specifically agreed terms, conditions, and safeguards designed to protect fundamental rights, health, and safety [Art. 58(4)].
* **Objectives:** Establishing these sandboxes aims to:
  * Improve legal certainty to achieve compliance with the Regulation or other relevant Union and national law [Art. 57(9)(a)];
  * Support the sharing of best practices through cooperation with involved authorities [Art. 57(9)(b)];
  * Foster innovation and competitiveness and facilitate the development of an AI ecosystem [Art. 57(9)(c)];
  * Contribute to evidence-based regulatory learning [Art. 57(9)(d)];
  * Facilitate and accelerate access to the Union market, particularly for systems provided by SMEs, including start-ups [Art. 57(9)(e)].
* **Establishment and Competence:** Member States must ensure their competent authorities establish at least one operational sandbox at national level by 2 August 2026 (either independently, jointly with other Member States, or by participating in an existing sandbox providing equivalent national coverage) [Art. 57(1)]. In addition, the European Data Protection Supervisor may establish an AI regulatory sandbox for Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies [Art. 57(3)]. 

Later amendments to the Act are not reflected in this text.

Under the EU AI Act, record-keeping requirements for high-risk AI systems include technical event-logging capabilities, documentation retention obligations, and quality management procedures:

**1. Automatic Event Logging**
* High-risk AI systems must technically enable the automatic recording of events (logs) over their lifetime [Art. 12(1)].
* To ensure traceability appropriate to the system's intended purpose, logging capabilities must record events relevant for:
  * identifying situations that may present a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or lead to a substantial modification [Art. 12(2)(a)];
  * facilitating post-market monitoring under Article 72 [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
  * monitoring operations referred to in Article 26(5) [Art. 12(2)(c)].
* Providers must keep the automatically generated logs when these are under their control [Art. 16(e)].
* For high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, logging capabilities must provide, at a minimum [Art. 12(3)]:
  * the period of each use (start and end date and time) [Art. 12(3)(a)];
  * the reference database checked against input data [Art. 12(3)(b)];
  * the input data that produced a match [Art. 12(3)(c)]; and
  * the identification of natural persons involved in verifying results under Article 14(5) [Art. 12(3)(d)].

**2. Documentation Keeping**
* Providers must keep the following documentation at the disposal of national competent authorities for a period ending 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)]:
  * technical documentation under Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * quality management system documentation under Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * documentation regarding changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * the EU declaration of conformity under Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)].
* Providers that are financial institutions subject to internal governance requirements under Union financial services law must maintain the technical documentation as part of documentation kept under that law [Art. 18(3)].

**3. Quality Management System**
* Providers must include "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" within their written quality management system [Art. 17(1)(k)].

Under the EU AI Act, the role of the European Artificial Intelligence Board (the ‘Board’) is to "advise and assist the Commission and the Member States in order to facilitate the consistent and effective application of this Regulation" [Art. 66(e)], [Art. 66(f)]. 

To achieve this, the Board's tasks and functions include:

* **Issuing recommendations and written opinions:** Either at the Commission's request or on its own initiative, the Board may issue recommendations and written opinions on relevant implementation and application matters [Art. 66(e)]. This includes topics such as:
  * The development and application of codes of conduct, codes of practice, and Commission guidelines [Art. 66(e)(i)];
  * The evaluation and review of the Regulation, serious incident reports, the functioning of the EU database, delegated or implementing acts, and alignment with Union harmonisation legislation listed in Annex I [Art. 66(e)(ii)];
  * Technical specifications, existing standards regarding Chapter III, Section 2 requirements, and the use of harmonised standards or common specifications [Art. 66(e)(iii)], [Art. 66(e)(iv)];
  * Trends in European global competitiveness in AI, AI uptake, digital skills development, and evolving AI value chain typologies regarding accountability implications [Art. 66(e)(v)], [Art. 66(e)(vi)];
  * Potential needs to amend Annex III or revise Article 5 based on available evidence and technology developments [Art. 66(e)(vii)].
* **Promoting awareness and literacy:** Supporting the Commission in promoting AI literacy, public awareness, and understanding of the benefits, risks, safeguards, rights, and obligations concerning AI systems [Art. 66(f)].
* **Facilitating cooperation through sub-groups:** Establishing two standing sub-groups to provide a platform for cooperation and exchange between market surveillance authorities (acting as the administrative cooperation group/ADCO) and notifying authorities regarding notified bodies, as well as creating other standing or temporary sub-groups to examine specific issues [Art. 65(6)].

When applying to a notified body for the assessment of technical documentation under the conformity assessment procedure in Annex VII, the application lodged by the provider must include the following [Annex VII, point 4.1], [Annex VII, point 4.2]:

* The name and address of the provider [Annex VII, point 4.2(a)];
* A written declaration that the same application has not been lodged with any other notified body [Annex VII, point 4.2(b)];
* The technical documentation referred to in Annex IV [Annex VII, point 4.2(c)].

Regarding the technical documentation itself, it must contain at least the elements specified in Annex IV [Art. 11(1)], [Annex IV], including:
* Validation and testing procedures, data characteristics, metrics, test logs, signed test reports, and cybersecurity measures put in place [Annex IV, point 2(g)–(h)];
* A copy of the EU declaration of conformity [Annex IV, points 8–9];
* A detailed description of the post-market performance evaluation system, including the post-market monitoring plan [Annex IV, points 8–9].

*(Note: SMEs, including start-ups, may provide the Annex IV technical documentation elements in a simplified manner using a form established by the Commission, which notified bodies must accept [Art. 11(1)].)*

High-risk AI systems must be accompanied by instructions for use provided "in an appropriate digital format or otherwise that include concise, complete, correct and clear information that is relevant, accessible and comprehensible to deployers" [Art. 13(2)]. 

The instructions for use must contain at least the following information:

* **Provider details:** The identity and contact details of the provider and, where applicable, its authorised representative [Art. 13(3)(a)].
* **System characteristics, capabilities, and performance limitations:**
  * Its intended purpose [Art. 13(3)(b)(i)];
  * The level of accuracy, including metrics, robustness, and cybersecurity against which it has been tested and validated and which can be expected, as well as any known and foreseeable circumstances that may impact those expected levels [Art. 13(3)(b)(ii)], [Art. 15(3)];
  * Any known or foreseeable circumstances—related to its intended purpose or reasonably foreseeable misuse—that may lead to risks to health and safety or fundamental rights [Art. 13(3)(b)(iii)];
  * Where applicable, technical capabilities and characteristics to provide information relevant to explaining its output [Art. 13(3)(b)(iv)];
  * When appropriate, performance regarding specific persons or groups of persons on which it is intended to be used [Art. 13(3)(b)(v)];
  * When appropriate, specifications for input data or relevant information on the training, validation, and testing data sets used [Art. 13(3)(b)(vi)];
  * Where applicable, information enabling deployers to interpret and appropriately use the output [Art. 13(3)(b)(vii)].
* **Pre-determined changes:** Any changes to the system and its performance pre-determined by the provider at the time of the initial conformity assessment [Art. 13(3)(c)].
* **Human oversight measures:** The human oversight measures referred to in Article 14, including technical measures implemented to help deployers interpret the system's outputs [Art. 13(3)(d)].
* **Resources and maintenance:** Computational and hardware resources needed, expected lifetime, and necessary maintenance and care measures (including frequency and software updates) to ensure proper functioning [Art. 13(3)(e)].
* **Logging mechanisms:** Where relevant, a description of mechanisms within the system allowing deployers to properly collect, store, and interpret logs in accordance with Article 12 [Art. 13(3)(f)].

